\title{\sffamily Internship report: Study of optimization-inspired network architectures for image generation with normalising flows}
\author{
Estéban Collas$^{\star}$, Audrey Repetti$^{\dagger}$
\\[5mm]
\small
\small $^\star$ Computer Science Department, ENS de Lyon, Lyon, France\\
\small $^\dagger$ School of Engineering and Physical Sciences, Heriot-Watt University, Edinburgh, UK
}
\date{}

% The title block is now carried by the title page above; uncomment \maketitle
% to get the article-style header back (e.g. for a paper version).
% \maketitle
\thispagestyle{empty}

\vskip 8mm

\begin{abstract}
Flow Matching (FM) achieves state-of-the-art performance in image generation, and
its links with diffusion and denoising are now well described in the literature.
Exploiting this equivalence, we study unfolded (or unrolled) neural networks (UNN)
--- light and robust architectures obtained by unrolling proximal algorithms
designed for Gaussian denoising --- as velocity estimators for FM. We show that an
unfolded strongly-convex Chambolle--Pock network, once conditioned on the noise
level, generates samples on 2-moons, MNIST and \TODO{[CIFAR-10]}. Beyond sample
quality, we argue that such architectures bring an interpretability that is out of
reach of the usual backbones: because they alternate between a primal variable that
remains a full-resolution image and a dual variable, every internal state of the
network can be displayed, and the denoising performed at each layer of each
integration step can be monitored directly. We finally discuss the limitations of
unfolded architectures for generation, in particular the expressivity cost of
keeping the primal iterate in image space.
\end{abstract}

{\bfseries Keywords.}
Flow Matching, unrolled neural networks, proximal algorithms, primal--dual
splitting, Chambolle--Pock, generative modelling, interpretability.

{\bfseries MSC.}
68T07, 49M29, 90C25, 94A08.

\tableofcontents

%=============================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Generative modelling of images is increasingly dominated by two closely related
families of methods: diffusion models and Flow Matching (FM). Both build a
transport between a simple reference distribution (typically a standard Gaussian)
and the data distribution, and both reduce, at the level of a single time step,
to a denoising problem: predicting a clean signal from a noisy observation. This
denoising equivalence, made precise for FM by \cite{gagneux2025avisualdive,
gagneux2025generation}, is the starting point of this internship.

Almost all FM and diffusion backbones used in practice are large convolutional or
attention-based black boxes (UNets, DiTs), chosen for their raw sample quality
rather than for any structural relationship to the underlying denoising problem.
In parallel, the signal-processing community has developed \emph{unrolled} (or
\emph{unfolded}) neural networks: architectures obtained by truncating a
convergent proximal algorithm for a variational denoising problem and letting its
parameters be learned end to end \cite{monga2021algorithm, gregor2010learning}.
These networks are considerably lighter than standard CNNs at comparable
denoising performance, and inherit Lipschitz robustness certificates from the
optimisation literature \cite{le2024unfolded, pesquet2021learning}.

The question addressed by this internship is whether this second family --
interpretable, certifiable, but so far confined to inverse problems such as
Gaussian denoising -- can be used as the velocity estimator of an FM generative
model, and at what cost in generation quality. This is, to our knowledge, an
unexplored combination at the start of the internship (\Cref{sec:related}): UNNs
had been studied extensively for denoising, and FM extensively with black-box
backbones, but not together.

Our contribution (\Cref{sec:contribution}) is threefold. First, we make the
denoising/FM equivalence operational: given any proximal denoiser, we describe
exactly how to turn it into an FM velocity field, and how to condition it on the
noise level. Second, we instantiate this with an unrolled strongly-convex
Chambolle--Pock network (ConvScCP) and study it on 2-moons, MNIST and CIFAR-10,
comparing it to UNet baselines under matched Flow Matching training. Third, we
show that the architecture is genuinely interpretable -- every internal state is
a displayable image -- and use this to observe how the network denoises
internally, while also identifying and correcting a structural limitation (an
odd-symmetry constraint) and reporting the negative results that shaped the
final design (\Cref{subsec:whatdidnotwork}). We close
(\Cref{sec:advantages_limits,sec:conclusion}) with a discussion of the
expressivity trade-off this interpretability comes with, and directions for
future work.

%=============================================================
\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:preliminaries}

This section collects the background material -- convex analysis, proximal
algorithms, unrolled networks and Conditional Flow Matching -- needed to state
our contribution. None of the results in this section are original; each is
attributed to its source.

\subsection{Convex Analysis}

In this section, we summarize the fundamental mathematical tools and definitions from convex analysis and vector calculus required to formalize unrolled architectures and flow matching objectives. Throughout this document, $\Hc$ denotes a real Hilbert space.

\begin{definition}[The class $\Gamma_0$]
We denote by $\Gamma_0(\Hc)$ the set of all proper, lower-semicontinuous, convex functions mapping $\Hc$ to $\RX$. A function $g$ is \textit{proper} if its domain $\dom (g) = \menge{x \in \Hc}{g(x) < \pinf}$ is non-empty.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fenchel-Legendre Conjugate]
Let $g \in \Gamma_0(\Hc)$. The Fenchel-Legendre conjugate of $g$ is the function $g^* \in \Gamma_0(\Hc)$ defined by:
\begin{equation}
    g^*(u) = \sup_{x \in \Hc} \left( \scal{x}{u} - g(x) \right).
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Quadratic Function]
Let $g(x) = \frac{1}{2}\|x\|^2$. Its conjugate is:
\begin{equation}
    g^*(u) = \frac{1}{2}\|u\|^2.
\end{equation}
More generally, if $g(x) = \frac{1}{2}\scal{Ax}{x}$ where $A$ is a continuous symmetric positive definite operator, then $g^*(u) = \frac{1}{2}\scal{u}{A^{-1}u}$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Norms and Dual Norms]
More generally, let $g(x) = \|x\|$ be a norm on $\Hc$. Its conjugate is the indicator function of the unit ball of the dual norm $\|\cdot\|_*$:
\begin{equation}
    g^*(u) = \delta_{\mathcal{B}_*}(u) =
    \begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if } \|u\|_* \leq 1, \\
    +\infty & \text{otherwise,}
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\|u\|_* = \sup \{ \scal{u}{x} : \|x\| \leq 1 \}$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Indicator and Support Functions]
Let $C \subseteq \Hc$ be a non-empty closed convex set. Let $g = \delta_C$ be the indicator function of $C$ (i.e., $g(x)=0$ if $x \in C$ and $+\infty$ otherwise). Its conjugate is the support function of $C$:
\begin{equation}
    g^*(u) = \sigma_C(u) = \sup_{x \in C} \scal{u}{x}.
\end{equation}
\end{example}

\begin{definition}[Proximal Operator]\label{proximal_operator}
Let $g \in \Gamma_0(\Hc)$. For any $\gamma \in \RPP$, the proximity operator of $\gamma g$ is the unique mapping $\prox_{\gamma g}: \Hc \to \Hc$ defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \prox_{\gamma g}(x) = \argmind{y \in \Hc}{\left( g(y) + \frac{1}{2\gamma} \| y - x \|^2 \right)}.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Moreau Decomposition]
Let $g \in \Gamma_0(\Hc)$ and $\gamma \in \RPP$. For any $x \in \Hc$, the following identity holds:
\begin{equation}
    \prox_{\gamma g}(x) = x - \gamma \prox_{\gamma^{-1} g^*}(\gamma^{-1} x).
\end{equation}
This formula is central to unrolling algorithms in the dual domain, as it allows expressing a proximal step as a residual connection (see [???]).
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Vector Calculus and Dynamical Systems}

The study of Continuous Normalizing Flows and Flow Matching requires defining operators on vector fields $v: \RR^d \times [0, 1] \to \RR^d$ and probability densities $p_t: \RR^d \to \RR$.

\begin{definition}[Divergence]
The divergence of a differentiable vector field $v(x) = (v_1(x), \dots, v_d(x))$ is the scalar function defined by:
\begin{equation}
    \text{div}(v) = \nabla \cdot v = \sum_{i=1}^d \frac{\partial v_i}{\partial x_i}.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Continuity Equation]
A probability path $p_t(x)$ is generated by a time-dependent vector field $v_t(x)$ if it satisfies the continuity equation (also known as the transport equation):
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\partial p_t(x)}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot(p_t(x) v_t(x)) = 0.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Linear Algebra and Stability}

\begin{definition}[Spectral Norm]
For a linear operator $L: \RR^{N} \to \RR^{M}$, the spectral norm (or induced $L_2$ norm) is defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \| L \|_S = \sup_{x \neq 0} \frac{\| Lx \|_2}{\| x \|_2} = \sqrt{\lambda_{\max}(L^\top L)},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{\max}$ denotes the largest eigenvalue. \textit{This norm is used to provide certificates of robustness and convergence for unrolled neural networks (see [??]).}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Feedforward Networks}

Feedforward Neural Networks (FNNs), or Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs), represent the most fundamental architecture in deep learning. Their primary characteristic is that information flows in a single direction: from the input layer, through one or more hidden layers, to the output layer, without any cycles or loops.

\paragraph{Mathematical Formulation.}
An FNN can be viewed as a function $\mathcal{F}_\Theta$ that maps an input $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{in}}$ to an output $x_K \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}$ through a sequence of $K$ layer transformations. Each layer $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$ performs an affine transformation followed by a non-linear activation function $\sigma_k$:

\begin{equation}
    x_k = \sigma_k(W_k x_{k-1} + b_k)
\end{equation}

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $W_k \in \mathbb{R}^{N_k \times N_{k-1}}$ is the \textbf{weight matrix} of the $k$-th layer.
    \item $b_k \in \mathbb{R}^{N_k}$ is the \textbf{bias vector} (or shift parameter).
    \item $\sigma_k$ is the \textbf{activation function} (not necessarily applied element-wise), which introduces the non-linearity necessary for the network to approximate complex, non-convex functions. Common choices include ReLU (= $\max(0,.)$), Sigmoid, or GELU, applied element-wise.
\end{itemize}

The complete network is thus a composition of operators:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{F}_\Theta(x_0) = (L_K \circ L_{K-1} \circ \dots \circ L_1)(x_0)
\end{equation}
where $L_k(x) = \sigma_k(W_k x + b_k)$, and the set of all learnable parameters is $\Theta = \{W_k, b_k\}_{k=1}^K$.

\subsection{Proximal Algorithms}

Proximal algorithms come from the so-called "variational approach", which consists of adding a prior to an optimisation problem in order to solve it. More concretely, the starting point of the variational approach is typically an inverse problem, where one seeks to recover an underlying signal $\Bar{x}$ from a degraded measurement $z$. We will consider the case where:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:inv-pb}
    z = A\Bar{x} + \epsilon,
\end{equation}
with $A$ a linear operator, and $\epsilon \sim \Nc(0, \sigma^2I)$.

Without using training data, but knowing $A$, a natural way to recover $\Bar{x}$ would be to solve:

\begin{equation}
    \min_{x} \underbrace{\frac{1}{2} \| z - Ax \|_2^2}_{f_z(x)}
\end{equation}
However, this kind of problem is almost always ill-posed ($A$ is not injective). Hence, to get a better solution (e.g. a realistic denoised image), we add a prior $g$ on $x$ (ie., we use the variational approach). A common form for the prior leads to:
\begin{equation}\label{f_and_g}
    \min_{x} \underbrace{\frac{1}{2} \| z - Ax \|_2^2}_{f_z(x)} + \underbrace{\mu \varphi( W x)}_{g(x)}
\end{equation}
with $\varphi \in \Gamma_0(\Hc)$, $W$ a linear operator (ie. $W \in \Lc(\Hc)$), $\mu \geq 0$. Here, $f_z$ is the data fidelity term and $g \in \Gamma_0(\Hc)$ is a regularization term (prior) promoting specific properties (like sparsity if $\varphi$ is the $\ell_1$ norm). The operator $W$ is often a sparsifying transform (e.g., wavelets or finite differences). $\mu$ indicates how much regularization we want.

Problems such as (\ref{f_and_g}) are often solved using iterative algorithms of the form:
\begin{equation}\label{algo_prox}
    x_{k+1} = \mathcal{T}_k(x_k, z; \Theta)
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{T}_k$ are transition operators and $\Theta$ is a set of fixed (hyper)parameters (e.g., step sizes, regularization weights). Given appropriate assumptions, those algorithms are designed to converge to a solution of (\ref{f_and_g}) as $k \xrightarrow{} \infty$.

When $g$ is non-differentiable (eg. if $\varphi$ is the $\ell_1$ norm), standard gradient descent cannot be applied to solve (\ref{f_and_g}). Instead, the iterative algorithms we use contain proximal operators (see \ref{proximal_operator}). Hence, they are called "proximal algorithms".

\subsubsection{Some Proximal Algorithms}

Let $f, f_1, f_2, \varphi, g \in \Gamma_0(\Hc)$ and $W \in \Lc(\Hc)$.

\paragraph{Forward-Backward (FB) Algorithm.}
The FB algorithm \cite{combettes2005signal} is the standard method for solving $\min_x f(x) + g(x)$ (see (\ref{f_and_g})) when $f$ is convex and $L$-smooth (i.e., $\nabla f$ is $L$-Lipschitz). The update rule is:
\begin{equation}
    x_{k+1} = \prox_{\gamma g} (x_k - \gamma \nabla f(x_k))
\end{equation}
where $\gamma$ is the step size. If $\gamma \in (0, 2/L)$, $x_k$ converges weakly to a minimizer, and the objective value converges at a rate of $O(1/k)$.

\paragraph{Dual Forward-Backward (Dual FB).}
When the prior is complex (e.g., $ g = \mu \cdot \varphi \circ W$ where $W$ is not easily invertible), it is often more efficient to solve the dual problem. The classical form of Dual FB iterations \cite{le2024unfolded}, for $\min_x f(x) + \frac{1}{2}\|x-z\|^2 + \varphi(Wx)$, leads to:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{cases}
    x_{k+1} = \prox_{f} (z - W^*u_{k}) \\
    u_{k+1} = \prox_{\tau \varphi^*} (u_k + \tau W x_{k+1}) \\
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\varphi^*$ is the Fenchel conjugate of $\varphi$, and $\tau > 0$. Convergence of $x_k$ is guaranteed if $\tau \in (0, 2/ \|W\|^2)$.

\paragraph{Chambolle-Pock (CP) Algorithm.}
The Chambolle-Pock algorithm \cite{chambolle2011first} is designed for problems of the form $\min_x f(x) + \varphi(Wx)$, where both $f$ and $\varphi$ are potentially non-smooth but have simple proximal operators. The iterations are:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{cases}
    u_{k+1} = \prox_{\sigma \varphi^*} (u_k + \sigma W \tilde{x}_k) \\
    x_{k+1} = \prox_{\tau f} (x_k - \tau W^* u_{k+1}) \\
    \tilde{x}_{k+1} = 2x_{k+1} - x_k
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
The algorithm converges to a saddle point of the primal-dual problem provided that the step sizes satisfy $\tau \sigma \|W\|^2 < 1$. Unlike FB, CP does not require any function to be differentiable.

\paragraph{Strongly-Convex Chambolle--Pock (ScCP).}
The ScCP algorithm \cite{chambolle2011first} is the accelerated variant of CP, exploiting the strong convexity of the data-fidelity term $f_z(x) = \frac{1}{2}\|z - x\|^2$.
Adapting the formulation from \cite{le2024unfolded} to our unconstrained setting and denoting the primal and dual stepsizes as $\tau_k$ and $\sigma_k$ respectively, the associated iterations read, for $k = 0, 1, \ldots$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sccp}
    \begin{cases}
        \displaystyle
        x_{k+1} = \frac{1}{1+\tau_k}\bigl(x_k - \tau_k W^\top u_k\bigr) + \frac{\tau_k}{1+\tau_k}\, z, \\[10pt]
        \displaystyle
        u_{k+1} = \prox_{\sigma_k g^*}\!\left(
            u_k + \sigma_k W\bigl[(1+\alpha_k)\,x_{k+1} - \alpha_k\, x_k\bigr]
        \right),
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $x_0 = z$, $u_0 = 0$, and $\alpha_k$ is a momentum parameter. Note that when $\alpha_k = 1$, \eqref{eq:sccp} reduces to standard CP; and when $\alpha_k = 0$, it recovers the Arrow--Hurwicz algorithm \cite{arrow1958}.

In the accelerated ScCP scheme, the momentum schedule is driven by the strong convexity constant of $f_z$, which is exactly $1$; we therefore fix $\rho = 1$ throughout, so that $\alpha_k = (1 + 2\tau_k)^{-1/2}$. If $(\tau_k)_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ and $(\sigma_k)_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ are positive sequences satisfying $\tau_0\sigma_0\|W\|_2^2 \leq 1$, with $\tau_{k+1} = \alpha_k\tau_k$ and $\sigma_{k+1} = \sigma_k/\alpha_k$, the sequence converges to the exact solution $\hat{x} = \lim_{k\to\infty} x_k$ \cite{chambolle2011first}.

\subsubsection{A simple case: ISTA}\label{ISTA}

To illustrate this, consider the sparse coding problem, which aims to find a sparse vector $x$ such that $z \approx Ax$ for a given dictionary $A$. This is formulated as the LASSO problem:
\begin{equation}\label{ISTA_eq}
    \min_{x} \underbrace{\frac{1}{2} \| z - Ax \|_2^2}_{f_z(x)} + \underbrace{\mu \| x \|_1}_{g(x)}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{ISTA.}
The Iterative Soft-Thresholding Algorithm (ISTA) \cite{beck2009fast} solves (\ref{ISTA_eq}) by alternating between a gradient descent step on the data fidelity term and a proximal mapping (soft-thresholding) for the $L_1$ penalty:
\begin{equation}
    x_{k+1} = \text{prox}_{\frac{\mu}{L}||.||} \left( x_k - \frac{1}{L} A^T (Ax_k - z) \right)
\end{equation}
where $L$ is a Lipschitz constant of $\nabla f_z$, and the soft-thresholding operator $\text{prox}_{\lambda||.||} = \text{sign}(s) \max(|s| - \lambda, 0) =: \eta_{\lambda}$ is applied element-wise.

\begin{remark}
    In this case, the smallest Lipschitz constant of $\nabla f_z$ is $\lambda_{max}(A^TA)$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
    It is FB with $W=I$ and $\varphi = \mu \|\cdot\|_1$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
We can rewrite this update as:
\begin{equation}\label{ISTA_as_NN}
    x_{k+1} = \eta_{\lambda} \left(W_1 x_k + W_2 z\right)
\end{equation}
with $W_1 = I - \frac{1}{L}A^T A$, $W_2 = \frac{1}{L}A^T$ and $\lambda = \frac{\mu}{L}$.

In this form, ISTA has the form of a feed-forward neural network. This remark is the basis of Unrolled Neural Networks.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Unrolled Neural Networks (UNN)}
Unrolled Neural Networks, also known as algorithm unrolling or deep unrolling, defines a class of machine learning models obtained by interpreting the iterations of an optimization algorithm as the layers of a neural network \cite{monga2021algorithm}. This makes them more interpretable and often more efficient than "black-box" architectures such as standard CNNs or Transformers.

Starting from an algorithm of the form of (\ref{algo_prox}), a UNN is constructed by:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Truncation:} Fixing the algorithm to a finite number of iterations $K$.
    \item \textbf{Parametrization:} Replacing the fixed parameters $\Theta$ with learnable weights $\theta_k$ for each layer $k$.
    \item \textbf{End-to-End Training:} Optimizing $\Theta = \{\theta_k\}_{k=1}^K$ using backpropagation on a dataset of $(z, x_{true})$ pairs, using some loss appropriate to our optimization problem.
\end{enumerate}

This results in a network where each layer corresponds to a single iteration of the original algorithm, but the parameters of the "solver" are optimized to work with $K$ iterations, and to solve a different optimization problem, defined using the specific distribution of the training data (see \cite{gregor2010learning}, \cite{le2024unfolded}, \cite{repetti_eusipco}, \cite{monga2021algorithm} for more details). For Gaussian denoising, such networks reach performances comparable to established denoising networks with orders of magnitude fewer parameters, while admitting Lipschitz certificates of robustness \cite{le2024unfolded, pesquet2021learning}.

\subsubsection{Example: LISTA}

For example, if one wants to recover $\Bar{x}$ from (\ref{eq:inv-pb}) using training data $(x_i, z_i)_i$, a natural loss is the MSE loss:

\begin{equation}\label{MSE_loss}
    \mathcal{L}_{MSE}(\hat{x}) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left( \frac{1}{2}||x_i - \hat{x}(z_i)||_2^2\right)
\end{equation}

\begin{remark}
An optimal estimator for (\ref{MSE_loss}) is a maximum likelihood estimator, in the case $\epsilon \sim \Nc(0,\sigma^2I)$.
\end{remark}

From here, one could use any architecture to model $\Hat{x}(.)$. An appropriate (see remark \ref{appropriate_meaning}) UNN would be LISTA:

\paragraph{LISTA.}
The \textit{Learned Iterative Soft-Thresholding Algorithm} (LISTA) \cite{gregor2010learning} transforms each ISTA iteration into a neural network layer. Starting from ISTA seen as in (\ref{ISTA_as_NN}), instead of fixing $W_1$ and $W_2$ based on the matrix $A$, LISTA treats them as learnable weight matrices. LISTA also learns $\lambda$:
\begin{equation}
    x_{k+1} = \eta_{\lambda_k} \left(W_{1,k} x_k +  W_{2,k} z \right)
\end{equation}
Using this architecture, the network can now learn the optimal operators to reconstruct $x$ from $z$, by solving \ref{MSE_loss} \cite{le2024unfolded}.

In other words, for the learning process, we use the MSE loss:

\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}(\Theta) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left( \frac{1}{2}||x_i - \text{LISTA}_{\Theta}(z_i)||_2^2\right)
\end{equation}

with $(x_i,z_i)$ our data points and $\Theta = \{\lambda_k, W_{1,k}, W_{2,k}\}$.

In general, unrolled algorithms do not return the convergence point of the iterative algorithm they are based on, because they are trained to solve a loss different than \ref{f_and_g}. However, when using ISTA's convergence points as "true" data points $x_i$, LISTA is roughly 20 times faster than FISTA for approximate solutions \cite{gregor2010learning}. Also, because the architecture is constrained by the physics of the problem, it generalizes better than pure black-box models, especially when the amount of training data is limited.

There are many possible implementations of UNN, for example if $A = BC$, we could choose to keep $B$ and to learn only $C$, or even learning $C^{T}$ independently from $C$, so introducing a mismatched adjoint (we already had a mismatched adjoint in the above example). We could also replace $\eta_{\lambda}$ by a more general neural network $\mathfrak{D}_{\lambda}$ (see \cite{repetti_eusipco} for more details).

\begin{remark}\label{appropriate_meaning}
    LISTA is "appropriate" for this optimization problem because ISTA solves a similar problem (see \ref{ISTA}). We can view LISTA as a way to learn the prior $g$ in ISTA.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Conditional Flow Matching (CFM)}

\textit{this section was heavily inspired by \cite{gagneux2025avisualdive}.}

Conditional Flow Matching (CFM) \cite{lipman2023flowmatching} is a simulation-free method for training Continuous Normalizing Flows (CNFs). It overcomes the primary bottleneck of standard CNFs, which is the need to solve an Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE) during each training iteration, by regressing a vector field against analytically defined conditional vector fields.

\subsubsection{Background: Continuous Normalizing Flows}
Let's say we want to sample from an unknown distribution $p_1$, using data points generated following $p_1$ (e.g., images of cats). CNFs allow us to do this sampling by following a probability path between a known distribution $p_0$ (e.g., a standard Gaussian) and $p_1$ \cite{NEURIPS2018_69386f6b}.

More precisely, a CNF learns a vector field $v_t(x): \mathbb{R}^d \times [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}^d$ that generates a probability path $p_t$ between $p_0$ and $p_1$ via the continuity equation:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial p_t(x)}{\partial t} = -\nabla \cdot (p_t(x) v_t(x))
\end{equation}

There are many paths between $p_0$ and $p_1$. When we use CFM for training a CNF, we start by choosing one of them (see \Cref{gaussian_paths}). For now, we consider $p_0$, $v_t$ fixed. In theory, given $p_0$ and $v_t$, one can use integration schemes, such as the Euler scheme, to sample from $p_1$. However, $v_t$ is often intractable.

The goal of flow matching is to find a neural network $v_\theta(x, t)$ that approximates the chosen vector field $v_t(x)$ (hence allowing us to use integration schemes).

\subsubsection{The Flow Matching Objective}
The ideal objective would be to minimize the Flow Matching (FM) loss:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{FM}}(\theta) := \mathbb{E}_{t \sim \mathcal{U}[0, 1], x \sim p_t(x)} \left[ \| v_\theta(x, t) - v_t(x) \|^2 \right]
\end{equation}
However, this objective is intractable because we typically do not have access to the marginal density $p_t(x)$ nor the marginal vector field $v_t(x)$ in closed form.

\subsubsection{The Conditional Approach}
CFM circumvents this by introducing a conditioning variable $z \sim q(z)$ independent of $t$ (most commonly a data sample $z = x_1 \sim p_{1} \text{\, or \,} z = (x_0,x_1) \sim p_0 \times p_{1}$, where we approximate $p_1$ with $p_{data}$ in practice). We define a \textit{conditional probability path} $p_t(x|z)$ and a corresponding \textit{conditional vector field} $v_t(x|z)$ that satisfy the following equations:
\begin{equation}\label{init_0}
\forall x, \mathbb{E}_z[p(x|z,t=0)] = p_0(x)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{init_1}
\forall x, \mathbb{E}_z[p(x|z,t=1)] = p_1(x)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial p_t(x|z)}{\partial t} = -\nabla \cdot (p_t(x|z) v_t(x|z))
\end{equation}
By aggregating these conditional paths, we recover the marginal distribution:

\begin{equation}
    p_t(x) := \int p_t(x|z) q(z) dz
\end{equation}

Crucially, it has been proven \cite{gagneux2025avisualdive} that under the hypotheses above, the marginal vector field $v_t(x)$ can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}
v_t(x) = \mathbb{E}_{z|(x,t)}[v_t(x|z)]
\end{equation}

Hence, choosing $q(z)$ and $p_t(x|z)$ amounts to choosing the vector field $v_t(x)$.

Furthermore, this leads to the \textbf{Conditional Flow Matching} objective:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}(\theta) := \mathbb{E}_{t\sim \mathcal{U}[0, 1], z \sim q(z), x \sim p_t(x|z)} \left[ \| v_\theta(x, t) - v_t(x|z) \|^2 \right]
\end{equation}

One can show \cite{gagneux2025avisualdive} that
\begin{equation}\label{FM_CFM}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{FM}}(\theta) = \mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}(\theta) + \text{const.}
\end{equation}
Hence, we can minimize the intractable FM loss by minimizing the CFM loss, provided we can define $p_t(x|z)$ and $v_t(x|z)$ analytically. Now, all that remains is to choose the right conditional path.

\subsubsection{Gaussian Paths}\label{gaussian_paths}
A simple choice for the conditional path is to use dirac distributions \cite{gagneux2025avisualdive}. Given a noise sample $x_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$ and an independent data sample $x_1 \sim p_{1}(x_1)$, we define $z = (x_0, x_1)$. We choose the following path:
\begin{equation}
p_t(x|x_0, x_1) = \delta_{a_t}(x)
\end{equation}
where $a_t = (1-t)x_0 + tx_1$. It is easy to derive the corresponding vector field:
\begin{equation}
v_t(x|x_0, x_1) = x_1 - x_0
\end{equation}

With this conditional path, we get the following (still intractable) objective:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{t\sim \mathcal{U}[0, 1], (x_0,x_1) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I) \times p_{1}(x_1), x \sim p_t(x|z)} \left[ \| v_\theta(x, t) - (x_1 - x_0) \|^2 \right]
\end{equation}

Approximating $p_1$ with $p_{data}$, we get the following tractable objective (equation (\ref{FM_CFM}) becomes an approximation):
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{t\sim \mathcal{U}[0, 1], (x_0,x_1) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I) \times p_{data}(x_1), x \sim p_t(x|z)} \left[ \| v_\theta(x, t) - (x_1 - x_0) \|^2 \right]
\end{equation}

In this setting, the lines connecting noise to data learned by the model tend to be straight (see the visuals on \cite{gagneux2025avisualdive}). This "straightness" is a key advantage of CFM over Diffusion Models, as it allows for much more efficient inference using numerical ODE solvers with large step sizes.

In real settings, people tend to use $p_t(x|x_0, x_1) = \mathcal{N}(x|\mu_t, \sigma_t^2I)$, where $\sigma_t$ is small (can be constant) instead of $\sigma_t = 0$, as it leads to better empirical results \cite{tong2024improving}.

\subsubsection{Choice of the coupling}\label{subsec:coupling}

The pairs $(x_0, x_1)$ need not be drawn from the independent product
$p_0 \otimes p_1$. Minibatch optimal-transport couplings (OT-CFM)
\cite{tong2024improving} instead pair each noise sample with the data sample
assigned to it by the discrete OT plan computed within the minibatch. This
straightens the conditional paths and, as we report in \Cref{subsec:whatdidnotwork},
led to faster training on all three datasets \emph{once an implementation bug in
our own coupling code was corrected}.

This choice is not only empirical. Under an OT coupling with a deterministic Monge
map, the OT-CFM velocity field has recently been shown to be exactly a proximal
operator, namely the Brenier map \cite{fukumizu2026proximal}. The proximal
structure of the architectures studied here is therefore structurally aligned with
the target they are asked to regress, which is not the case under independent
coupling. \correc{This is an alignment of structure, not a proof that the unrolled
network recovers the Brenier map; we have not tested this claim.}

\subsubsection{Summary}

Flow Matching in practice:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Choose a variable $z$ with some known distribution $q(z)$
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{e.g., $z = (x_0, x_1)$, $q((x_0,x_1)) = p_0(x_0) \times p_1(x_1))$}
        \end{itemize}
    \item Choose a simple conditional distribution $p_t(x |z)$
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{e.g., $\delta_{(1-t)x_0 + tx_1}(x)$}
        \end{itemize}
    \item Compute an associated velocity field $v_{t}(x|z)$
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{e.g., $x_1 - x_0$}
        \end{itemize}
    \item Train model using conditional loss $\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}$
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{sample $t, z, x$ using the data points}
        \end{itemize}
    \item Sample from (a distribution close to) $p_1 \approx p_{data}$
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textit{sample $x_0 \sim p_0$, then use an integration scheme, e.g., Euler Scheme}
        \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}
A minimal reference implementation of this loop is given in \Cref{app:code}.

%=============================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

At the start of the internship, the two ingredients of our approach were well
developed separately but, to our knowledge, not combined.

On the unrolling side, Le, Repetti and Pustelnik \cite{le2024unfolded} study
unfolded proximal networks for robust image Gaussian denoising, establishing the
LNO/LFO learning strategies and the associated Lipschitz robustness certificates
that we adapt in \Cref{subsubsec:lno_lfo}. This line of work, together with the
original LISTA construction \cite{gregor2010learning} and the general algorithm-unrolling
survey \cite{monga2021algorithm}, treats UNNs purely as denoisers or
inverse-problem solvers: the noise level is either fixed or a nuisance parameter,
never a continuously varying conditioning signal driving a generative process.

On the Flow Matching side, the denoising reformulation of Gagneux et al.
\cite{gagneux2025avisualdive, gagneux2025generation} and the proximal-operator
viewpoint of Fukumizu et al. \cite{fukumizu2026proximal} establish, respectively,
that the optimal FM velocity is the MMSE denoiser at every noise level
(\Cref{eq:fm-denoise} below) and that under optimal-transport coupling the FM
velocity is itself a proximal operator (the Brenier map). Both results are stated
at the level of the \emph{optimal} velocity field; neither proposes a concrete
architecture that is itself proximal. Standard FM implementations, including the
\texttt{torchcfm} library and its UNet backbones \cite{tong2024improving} that we
use as baselines, instantiate $v_\theta$ with generic convolutional networks.

The gap we identified, and that motivates \Cref{sec:contribution}, is therefore:
no existing work instantiates the FM velocity field with a proximal unrolled
network, exploits the resulting proximal reading for interpretability, or
studies the trade-off against black-box UNet backbones under matched training. We
also note the structural relationship between our convolutional ScCP block and
multi-layer convolutional sparse coding (ML-CSC) \cite{papyan2017convolutional}:
our architecture corresponds to a single layer of the ML-CSC hierarchy, without
its nested outer loop over dictionary levels, a comparison we return to in
\Cref{subsubsec:vs_unet}.

%=============================================================
\section{Our Contribution}
\label{sec:contribution}

This section presents the contribution of the internship, in three movements.

\emph{The idea} (\Cref{subsec:denoising}): the optimal FM velocity field is, up to
an affine reparameterisation, an MMSE denoiser at a noise level indexed by $t$
(\Cref{subsubsec:duality}). This lets us place an unrolled proximal network at the
heart of the generative process (\Cref{subsec:ode_integration}), reduces the
estimation of $v_\theta(x_t,t)$ to a family of variational denoising problems
(\Cref{subsec:variational}), dictates the loss the network should be trained with
(\Cref{subsubsec:xpred}), and finally gives the sampling procedure
(\Cref{subsec:inference}).

\emph{The choices} (\Cref{subsec:strategies}): within that template, two design
axes remain open --- how the parameters of the unrolled algorithm are constrained
(LNO vs.\ LFO, \Cref{subsubsec:lno_lfo}) and what plays the role of the proximal
operator (\Cref{subsubsec:prox_choice}). We state each axis, the options we
compared, and the one we retained.

\emph{The implementation}
(\Cref{subsec:algo_params,subsec:models,subsec:hyperparams}): we then give the
exact unrolled recursions of the two algorithm families we built on
(\Cref{subsec:algo_params}), the catalogue of models we actually implemented and
trained, together with the architectural fix without which convolutional ScCP
cannot represent image data at all (\Cref{subsec:models}), and the training
hyperparameters (\Cref{subsec:hyperparams}).
\Cref{subsubsec:vs_unet} closes the section by contrasting the resulting
architecture with a UNet.

The numerical results obtained with these models are reported in
\Cref{sec:experiments}, the negative results that shaped the final design in
\Cref{subsec:whatdidnotwork}, and a discussion of advantages and limitations in
\Cref{sec:advantages_limits}.

\subsection{A Denoising Perspective on Flow Matching}\label{subsec:denoising}

\subsubsection{The denoising--velocity duality}
\label{subsubsec:duality}

In the context of generative models such as Flow Matching, the corrupted observation at time $t$ can be expressed as $x_t = t x_1 + (1-t) x_0$, where $x_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$ represents noise. From a denoising perspective, the objective of the model is to estimate the clean signal $x_1$ from the noisy measurement $x_t$.

\begin{remark}
We have $x_1 - x_0 = (x_1 - x_t)/(1 - t)$.
\end{remark}

By rescaling the problem to a standard additive noise model, we can write:
\begin{equation}\label{denoising_equ}
z = \frac{x_t}{t} = x_1 + \frac{1-t}{t} x_0 \implies z = x_1 + \epsilon_t, \quad \text{with } \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_t^2 I)
\end{equation}
where the effective noise variance is $\sigma_t^2 = \left( \frac{1-t}{t} \right)^2$. It is an inverse problem, with $A = Id$ (see \cite{gagneux2025generation}).

Following the Maximum A Posteriori (MAP) framework, the estimate $\hat{x}$ is obtained by maximizing the posterior distribution $p(x_1 | z) \propto p(z | x_1) p(x_1)$. Assuming a fixed prior $\alpha g$, the MAP estimator is the minimizer of the negative log-likelihood:
\begin{equation}
\hat{x} = \arg\min_{x} \left( \frac{\|z - x\|^2}{2\sigma_t^2} + \alpha g(x) \right)
\end{equation}
By multiplying the objective function by $\sigma_t^2$, we recover the standard variational form:
\begin{equation}
\hat{x} = \arg\min_{x} \left( \frac{1}{2} \|z - x\|^2 + \mu_t g(x) \right), \quad \text{with } \mu_t = \alpha \cdot \sigma_t^2
\end{equation}

This derivation proves that the optimal regularization parameter $\mu_t$ must be proportional to the variance of the noise present at time $t$.

Equivalently, and in the notation used in the rest of this report, combining
$x_t = (1-t)x_0 + t x_1$ with the optimal velocity
$v^\star(x_t,t) = \mathbb{E}[x_1 - x_0 \mid x_t, t]$ gives the identity
\cite[Formula (5)]{gagneux2025generation}
\begin{equation}
  D^\star_t(x_t) := \mathbb{E}[\,x_1 \mid x_t, t\,] = x_t + (1-t)\, v^\star(x_t, t),
  \label{eq:fm-denoise}
\end{equation}
i.e.\ the MMSE estimator of the clean image $x_1$ given the noisy observation $x_t$
and the noise level $t$. Thus the optimal FM velocity gives the optimal denoiser at
every noise level, and conversely any denoiser $D_t$ induces a velocity
$v(x,t) = (D_t(x) - x)/(1-t)$. This duality is what motivates the use of
denoising-oriented unfolded architectures for FM, and is the central idea of this
report.

\subsubsection{Role of the UNN}
\label{subsec:ode_integration}

Now that we know that we want to use an UNN for FM, two natural options are available to us. \emph{(i)} Each layer of the
UNN is interpreted as one Euler step of the generation ODE, so that an image is
generated from Gaussian noise in a single forward pass. \emph{(ii)} The UNN is
called at every time step of the integrator, and denoises the current noisy image
to give the direction in which to move next. We experimented with both and retained
option \emph{(ii)}, notably because one of the strengths of velocity modelling comes
from using the \emph{same} model to denoise at different noise levels
\cite{gagneux2025generation}.

\subsubsection{Recovering the velocity}
\label{subsec:variational}

As derived in \Cref{subsubsec:duality}, the vector field estimation at time $t \in \left]0,1\right]$
reduces to solving the following variational problem:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{x} = \underset{x \in \mathcal{H}}{\operatorname{argmin}} \;
    \frac{1}{2} \| z - x \|^2
    + \mu_t \, g(Wx),
    \label{eq:variational_prob}
\end{equation}
where $z = x_t / t$ is the rescaled noisy observation and $\mu_t = \alpha (1-t)^2/t^2$
is the time-dependent regularisation parameter, with $\alpha > 0$ a learnable global scalar.
The velocity field is then recovered via
\begin{equation}
    v_\theta(x_t, t) = \frac{\hat{x} - x_t}{1-t}.
\end{equation}

Hence, given a proximal algorithm $\Ac(\alpha,z)$ solving $\min_{x} \frac{1}{2} \| z - x \|_2^2 + \alpha g(x)$ (eg. DFB, ScCP, ...), we can use \Cref{Prox_for_vector_field} to get a proximal algorithm converging to the value of the vector field at any point $(x(t),t)$.

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Prox\_for\_vector\_field($\Ac, x_t, t, \alpha$)}\label{Prox_for_vector_field}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State $\mathrm{var} \gets ((1-t)/t)^2$ \Comment{noise \emph{variance} $\sigma_t^2$}

\State $\mu \gets \alpha \cdot \mathrm{var}$

\State $z \gets x_t/t$

\State $\Hat{x}_1 \gets \Ac(\mu,z)$

\Return $(\Hat{x}_1 - x_t)/(1-t)$

\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

As simple as this derivation may be, two numerical subtleties arise from this denoising reduction. First, the rescaling $z = x_t/t$ of Algorithm~\ref{Prox_for_vector_field} blows up as
$t \to 0$. In practice we cannot divide by t in our code. We therefore warm-start every unrolled network directly
from $z = x_t$ (instead of $xt/t$), which does not blow up near $0$. This seemed to us the simplest solution to our issue and has the advantage of allowing us to use our algorithm for any model based on a classical denoiser (ie. a denoiser designed to take as input $z = x^* + \epsilon$ and recover $x^*$), but has the downside of shifting us away from the mathematical foundations of our algorithm, because we just remove a division and let the network figure out what to do with its input. Another solution would have been to re-derive \ref{denoising_equ} and work on a different version of the ScCP algorithm designed to take $x_t$ as input. More on this in section [???].

Second, the final division by $1-t$ blows up as $t \to 1$; this issue is simpler to solve: at inference as well as during training, we always sample the time $t$ in $[0,t_{max}]$ (because we are using Euler discretization to solve the ODE). So the denominator is never too small, following \cite[Section 3.3]{gagneux2025generation}, \cite{li2026back}. We also experimented with applying $max(.,t_{max})$ on the denominator inside the network, which led to similar results, but does not maintain the equivalence between the denoising and the velocity modeling, so it was not preferred.

\subsubsection{Output parameterisation: $x$-prediction with a velocity-space loss}
\label{subsubsec:xpred}

The duality of \Cref{subsubsec:duality} is in fact more than a change of
viewpoint: it also fixes how the network should be trained. A model estimating
the vector field can be parameterised in three equivalent ways:
predicting the velocity $v$ itself, the denoised image $x_1$, or the source noise
$x_0$. It is unclear in the literature which is best in general. Since the
architecture used here has its roots in image denoising, and following
\cite{gagneux2025generation} and \cite{li2026back}, we predict $x_1$.

In \emph{velocity prediction} ($v$-pred) the network outputs the velocity
directly, $v_\theta(x_t,t)$, and is trained with the standard CFM loss
$\|v_\theta - (x_1-x_0)\|^2$. In \emph{clean-signal prediction} ($x$-pred) the
network instead outputs an estimate $D_\theta(x_t,t) \approx x_1$ of the clean
image, and the velocity is reconstructed \emph{a posteriori} via
$v_\theta(x_t,t) = (D_\theta(x_t,t) - x_t)/(1-t)$. This is the natural output of
our unrolled solvers, whose primal iterate $\hat{x}$ is by construction an
estimate of $x_1$ (the MAP denoiser of \Cref{subsec:denoising}); we flag such
models with the attribute \texttt{predicts\_x1}.

The two parameterisations are made \emph{exactly} equivalent by the choice of
loss. Using $x_1 - x_t = (1-t)(x_1 - x_0)$, the velocity error factorises as
\begin{equation}
    \bigl\| v_\theta(x_t,t) - (x_1-x_0) \bigr\|^2
    = \frac{1}{(1-t)^2}\,\bigl\| D_\theta(x_t,t) - x_1 \bigr\|^2 .
    \label{eq:vloss_equiv}
\end{equation}
Hence, rather than regressing $D_\theta$ onto $x_1$ with a plain
$\|D_\theta - x_1\|^2$ objective, we train it with the \emph{velocity-space
weighted} loss
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{x-pred}}(\theta)
    = \mathbb{E}_{t, x_0, x_1}\!\left[
        w(t)\,\bigl\| D_\theta(x_t,t) - x_1 \bigr\|^2
    \right],
    \qquad
    w(t) = \frac{1}{\max\!\bigl((1-t)^2,\ \varepsilon^2\bigr)},
    \label{eq:xpred_loss}
\end{equation}
with $\varepsilon = 0.05$. By \eqref{eq:vloss_equiv}, up to the clamp this is
\emph{identical} to the CFM objective $\mathbb{E}\|v_\theta-(x_1-x_0)\|^2$: the
network is optimised for the correct velocity-matching goal, while regressing a
target ($x_1$) that is bounded, well scaled and image-like at all times. The
clamp merely caps the weight in the immediate neighbourhood of $t=1$, where the
raw $1/(1-t)^2$ factor would otherwise diverge, trading a negligible bias for
stability.

This parameterisation is not cosmetic. A plain $x$-prediction loss
($w \equiv 1$) makes the regression target $x_1$ nearly independent of $t$ and,
for a low-variance data distribution (e.g.\ a single MNIST class), produces a
weak, poorly-conditioned gradient: the network minimises the loss by collapsing
onto the \emph{data mean} and ignoring its input $x_t$, yielding a constant
generator. The $1/(1-t)^2$ weighting (equivalently, matching in velocity space)
restores the informative high-$t$ regime and removes this collapse. This is the
$x$-prediction / $v$-loss formulation advocated in the ``back-to-basics'' line of
work on diffusion and flow parameterisations \cite{li2026back}, and it is the
setting used for all MNIST and CIFAR-10 experiments below.

\subsubsection{Inference}
\label{subsec:inference}

Once we have trained an UNN to model the vector field, at inference, we draw $x \sim \mathcal{N}(0,I)$ and integrate
$\dot{x}(t) = v_\theta(x(t),t)$ with an explicit Euler scheme over $N$ ($= 20$, so $t_{max} = 0.95$) steps. At each step, the current iterate plays the role of the noisy observation of
\eqref{eq:variational_prob}, i.e.\ we set $z = x_t$. The noise level is passed separately through $t$, on which the proximal module $\mathcal{N}_{\theta_{\text{prox}}}$ is conditioned. The network returns $\hat{x}_1 = D_\theta(x_t,t)$ after $K$ unrolled iterations, and the velocity is recovered through \eqref{eq:fm-denoise}.

\subsection{Design Choices}
\label{subsec:strategies}

The denoising reduction of \Cref{subsec:denoising} fixes \emph{what} the network
must compute, not \emph{how}. The architectures we focused on are UNNs derived
from ScCP and DFB; ScCP is the one we carried forward to images, not for its
convergence rate but for the structure of its iteration --- a strongly convex
data-fidelity term whose primal step re-injects the observation $z$ at every
layer and keeps the primal iterate in image space, at full resolution
(\Cref{subsubsec:vs_unet}). For each family we then experimented with many
variants; the two main axes are given below, each with the options we compared
and the one retained in the final setup.

\subsubsection{LNO vs.\ LFO}
\label{subsubsec:lno_lfo}

Following \cite{le2024unfolded}, every UNN is trained under two learning strategies for
its parameter set $\Theta = \{W_k, V_k, \tau_k, \sigma_k, \alpha, \ldots\}$, where $V_k$
is the learned counterpart of $W_k^\top$:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Learned Normalised Operators (LNO).}  Theoretical convergence conditions
          are enforced at each layer: the adjoint is exact ($V_k = W_k^\top$) and stepsizes
          are bounded by the spectral norm of $W_k$, computed via power iterations, but independent.

    \item \textbf{Learned Flexible Operators (LFO).}  The adjoint constraint is relaxed
          ($V_k \neq W_k^\top$), and stepsizes are dependent, but learned without spectral
          constraints.
\end{enumerate}

For ScCP, the flexibility
of LFO lies in the \emph{operators} rather than in the step-sizes. It is \emph{LNO}
that learns each primal step-size $\tau_k$ independently, compensating through the
per-layer normalisation $\sigma_k = 0.99\,(\tau_k\|W_k\|_S^2)^{-1}$, whereas LFO ties
the entire schedule $(\tau_k, \sigma_k, \alpha_k)$ to the single scalar $\tau_0$
through the acceleration recursion \eqref{eq:sccp-sched}.

% \subsubsection{Shared vs.\ Per-Layer Weights}
% In the \textbf{per-layer} setting, every iteration $k$ owns an independent copy of
% $(W_k, V_k, \tau_k, \sigma_k, \theta_{\text{prox},k})$, which allows maximum
% expressiveness at the cost of a parameter count that grows linearly with $K$.
% In the \textbf{shared} setting, a single set of parameters $(W, V, \tau, \sigma,
% \theta_{\text{prox}})$ is reused at every iteration; the network then defines a recurrent
% dynamical system and can be run for a \emph{variable} number of steps at inference time
% via \texttt{model(x\_t, t, n\_iter=N)}, without retraining.

\subsubsection{Choice of Proximal Operator}
\label{subsubsec:prox_choice}

In our experiments, the dual proximal operator is fixed to the analytical form
induced by the $\ell_1$-norm prior. Since $(\mu\|\cdot\|_1)^* = \delta_{\mathcal{B}_\infty(\mu)}$,
the dual proximal step reduces to a clipping operation:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{prox}_{\tau (\mu\|\cdot\|_1)^*}(u)
    = \operatorname{clip}\bigl(u,\,-r(t),\,r(t)\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $r(t) = \mathrm{softplus}(\mathrm{MLP}(t))$ is a learned, time-dependent radius
(module \texttt{L1ProxFlat} for flat models, \texttt{L1ProxConv} for convolutional ones).

Keeping the analytical operator is a deliberate choice in our final setup: the
expressivity of the model is then carried by the operators $W_k$ and $V_k$ rather
than by the nonlinearity, which is what preserves the proximal reading of the layer.
Together with the bias of \Cref{subsubsec:oddsym}, the implemented operator is
\verb|clamp(| $\cdot$ \verb| + b, MLP(t))|.

\subsection{Algorithm Parameterisations}
\label{subsec:algo_params}

We now detail the two algorithm families used as the backbone of our UNNs, together
with the exact parameterisations adopted in the LNO and LFO regimes.

\subsubsection{Dual Forward-Backward (DFB)}

The DFB unrolled iterations read, for $k = 0, 1, \ldots, K-1$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:dfb_iter}
    \begin{cases}
        x_{k+1} = z - V_k\, u_k, \\[4pt]
        u_{k+1} = \mathcal{N}_{\theta_{\text{prox}}}
            \!\left(u_k + \tau_k W_k x_{k+1},\; t\right),
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
initialised with $x_0 = z$ and $u_0 = 0$.

\paragraph{DFB-LFO.} The learnable parameters per layer are
$\Theta_k = \{W_k, V_k, \tau_k, \theta_{\text{prox},k}\}$,
where $W_k$ and $V_k$ are independent (mismatched adjoint), and $\tau_k =
\mathrm{softplus}(\hat\tau_k)$ with $\hat\tau_k$ initialised at $0.5$.

\paragraph{DFB-LNO.} The adjoint is matched: $V_k = W_k^\top$. The stepsize is fixed
to $\tau_k = 1.99\,\|W_k\|_S^{-2}$, where $\|W_k\|_S$ is estimated by power
iteration at each forward pass ($n_{\text{iter}}=3$).

\subsubsection{Strongly-Convex Chambolle--Pock (ScCP)}

The ScCP scheme exploits the strong convexity of the data-fidelity term
$f_z(x)=\frac{1}{2}\|z-x\|^2$ to derive an accelerated primal-dual algorithm.
Its iterations read:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sccp_iter}
    \begin{cases}
        \displaystyle
        x_{k+1} = \frac{x_k - \tau_k V_k u_k + \tau_k z}{1 + \tau_k}, \\[8pt]
        \displaystyle
        y_{k+1} = x_{k+1} + \alpha_k (x_{k+1} - x_k), \\[6pt]
        \displaystyle
        u_{k+1} = \mathcal{N}_{\theta_{\text{prox}}}
            \!\left(u_k + \sigma_k W_k y_{k+1},\; t\right),
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
initialised with $x_0 = z$, $u_0 = 0$. The momentum $\alpha_k$ and the schedule of
$(\tau_k, \sigma_k)$ follow the accelerated recursion, with the strong-convexity
constant of $f_z$ fixed to $1$:
\begin{equation}
    \alpha_k = \left(1 + 2\,\tau_k\right)^{-1/2},
    \qquad
    \tau_{k+1} = \alpha_k \tau_k,
    \qquad
    \sigma_{k+1} = \frac{\sigma_k}{\alpha_k}.
    \label{eq:sccp-sched}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{ScCP-LFO.} Only $\tau_0$ is learned (initialised via
$\tau_0 = \mathrm{softplus}(\hat\tau_0)$, $\hat\tau_0 = -0.5$); the full
$(\tau_k, \sigma_k, \alpha_k)$ sequence is then derived recursively from
\eqref{eq:sccp-sched}. $W_k$ and $V_k$ are learned independently; $\sigma_0 = 1$
(absorbed into $W_k$).

\paragraph{ScCP-LNO.} Each $\tau_k$ is learned independently,
$\tau_k = \mathrm{softplus}(\hat\tau_k)$; $V_k = W_k^\top$; and
$\sigma_k = 0.99\,(\tau_k\,\|W_k\|_S^2)^{-1}$, ensuring the product
$\tau_k\sigma_k\|W_k\|_S^2 = 0.99 < 1$ at every layer, so that each layer is a
valid ScCP step in isolation. The momentum $\alpha_k$ is derived from $\tau_k$ as
above.

\subsection{Implemented Models}
\label{subsec:models}

We now list the models that were actually implemented and trained. All UNN
architectures share the following design conventions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Time conditioning.} If the proximal operator is an MLP, $t$ is
          concatenated directly to the input of the MLP. For convolutional or $\ell_1$ proximal operator, a small MLP maps $t \mapsto$ a channel-wise bias.
    \item \textbf{Truncation depth.} $K \in \{3, 5, 10, 15\}$ for our models.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Baselines}

Four reference deep-learning models are included:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{MLP\_baseline}: time-conditioned MLP, hidden width $w = 1024$.
    \item \texttt{UNet\_torchCFM\_baseline}: built-in UNet from the \texttt{torchcfm} library.
    \item \texttt{UNet\_baseline}: custom UNet, base channel count $\texttt{base\_ch} = 32$.
    \item \texttt{SmallUNet\_baseline}: lighter UNet, $\texttt{base\_ch} = 32$.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Standard UNNs}

Each iteration $k$ has its own independent parameters
$\{W_k \in \mathbb{R}^{d_u \times d}, V_k \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d_u}, \tau_k, \sigma_k,
\theta_{\text{prox},k}\}$, with $d = \dim(\mathcal{H})$ and $d_u = d$ (same-dimension dual).
The proximal operator is a small time-conditioned MLP with hidden width $w = 32$.
Three families are evaluated: \texttt{DFB\_UNN}, \texttt{DiFB\_UNN} and
\texttt{ScCP\_UNN}, each in LFO and LNO versions.

\subsubsection{Convolutional UNNs}
\label{subsubsec:convolutional}

For image data the dense operator $W_k$ is replaced by a learnable 2D
convolution and $V_k$ by a transposed convolution of matching shape. Concretely,
$W_k$ maps the primal image ($1$ channel for MNIST, $3$ for CIFAR-10) to a
$d_u = \texttt{ic}$-channel dual feature space with a $9\times 9$ kernel and padding $4$ (so that
$\texttt{conv\_transpose2d}$ is the exact adjoint of $\texttt{conv2d}$ and spatial
size is preserved), where the number of intermediate channels $\texttt{ic}$ is a
hyperparameter. The convolution weights are Kaiming-initialised. Unless stated
otherwise the proximal operator is always the analytical $\ell_1$ dual prox
(\texttt{L1ProxConv}). Two
families are provided: \texttt{ConvDFB\_UNN} and
\texttt{ConvScCP\_UNN}, each in LFO and LNO versions. For a description of all the variants implemented, see \Cref{app:variants}.

% complete list of the
% $40$ architecture variants we implemented, spanning flat, convolutional and
% weight-shared versions of all three algorithm families, is given in
% \Cref{app:variants} for reference.

\subsubsection{Breaking the odd symmetry of the proximal velocity field}
\label{subsubsec:oddsym}

The convolutional ScCP block with the analytical $\ell_1$ dual prox has a
structural property that is invisible on the (centred, near-symmetric) two-moons
data but harmful on images: as a function of its input, the induced velocity
field is \emph{odd}. Indeed, with warm start $z = x_t$, dual initialisation
$u_0 = 0$, and no convolutional bias, every ScCP update
(primal step, extrapolation, dual forward step) is \emph{linear} in $(x,u,z)$,
and the symmetric clipping prox satisfies $\mathrm{prox}(-a) = -\mathrm{prox}(a)$.
By induction the map $x_t \mapsto \hat{x}$ is odd, hence
\begin{equation}
    v_\theta(-x_t, t) = -\,v_\theta(x_t, t) .
    \label{eq:oddness}
\end{equation}
This holds \emph{architecturally}, even at initialisation and before any
training; we verify it numerically ($\|v_\theta(-x_t)+v_\theta(x_t)\|/\|v_\theta(x_t)\|
\approx 10^{-6}$).

Property \eqref{eq:oddness} propagates through the generative ODE:
$\mathrm{gen}(-x_0) = -\mathrm{gen}(x_0)$. On MNIST, a digit has a black
background ($\approx -1$) and white strokes, so its negation is a
\emph{colour-inverted} image (white background). Since the noise $x_0$ and $-x_0$
are equally likely, the generator is forced to produce a distribution that is
symmetric about $0$: roughly half of the generated samples come out
colour-inverted, and the model cannot represent the asymmetric MNIST
distribution. This is a genuine limitation inherited from the odd symmetry of the
underlying convex/proximal problem.

We break the symmetry with a \textbf{learned convolutional bias (\texttt{w\_bias})}.
A per-dual-channel bias $b$ is added in the dual forward operator,
$\sigma_k\,(W_k y_{k+1} + b)$, which amounts to penalising $g(W_k\,\cdot - b)$
instead of $g(W_k\,\cdot)$. The shift makes the map non-odd in $x_t$. With $b$
zero-initialised the model starts exactly at the symmetric operator.

This fix preserves the proximal/variational interpretation of the layer (it
corresponds to a shifted argument) while removing the parity constraint that made
unmodified ScCP unable to fit image data.

\subsection{Structural Differences with a UNet}
\label{subsubsec:vs_unet}

Our convolutional ScCP differs from a UNet in three ways, all three of which matter
for the interpretation of \Cref{subsec:interp}.

\paragraph{No multiscale.} A UNet builds its receptive field by successive
downsampling, so intermediate activations live on coarser grids and are no longer
images. ScCP has no pooling: the primal iterate $x_k$ always stays at full
resolution, and its receptive field grows only \emph{linearly} with the number of
unrolled iterations $K$. This is what makes the internal states directly
displayable. \correc{This locality does not appear to limit generation as long as
the network has enough layers, but we have not tested this systematically.}

\paragraph{Primal--dual alternation with input re-injection.} A UNet propagates a
single stream of feature maps, with skip connections carrying resolution across the
encoder--decoder. ScCP instead alternates between a few-channel primal variable in
image space (one channel for MNIST, three for CIFAR-10) and a multi-channel dual
variable, and re-injects the observation $z$ at \emph{every} primal step (see
\eqref{eq:sccp_iter}). The architecture therefore anchors the primal variable to the
input at each step, rather than progressively constructing an abstracted
representation of it.

\paragraph{Nonlinearity.} The only nonlinearity in ScCP is a proximal operator ---
here a clipping with a learned, time-dependent radius --- whereas a UNet stacks
pointwise activations together with normalisation layers, which aggregate spatial
statistics over the whole feature map. Every ScCP layer is thus a strictly local,
translation-equivariant operation. We also note that convolutional ScCP corresponds
to a single layer of the multi-layer convolutional sparse coding (ML-CSC) hierarchy
of Papyan, Romano and Elad \cite{papyan2017convolutional}, lacking its nested outer
loop over dictionary levels.

\paragraph{No attention and no batchnorm.} In the torchCFM UNet, Attention Blocks are used to improve the performance of the architecture. We did not use attention in our models, nor Batchnorm.

\subsection{Hyperparameters}
\label{subsec:hyperparams}

All models are optimised with Adam (learning rate $10^{-3}$) via the standard
CFM loss $\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}(\theta)$, using Minibatch Optimal Transport couplings
\cite{tong2024improving}. Dataset-specific settings are as follows: batch size $256$ and
$20\,000$ iterations on \textit{two moons}; batch size $128$ on MNIST.

