\section{Explored Directions}
\label{sec:explored}

The architecture reported in \Cref{sec:experiments} is the end point of a much
wider exploration. This section documents that exploration: the dead ends that
shaped the final design, the side investigations that answered a question without
changing the architecture, and the questions we opened but could not close. We
consider this material to be part of the contribution of the internship rather
than an appendix to it: several of the results below are negative results about
claims that looked plausible \emph{a priori}, including two of our own.

Throughout, each item ends with an explicit verdict, using the following
convention: \emph{confirmed} means measured and reproduced under a control;
\emph{refuted} means the hypothesis was ours (or the literature's) and the
measurement contradicted it; \emph{inconclusive} means the measurement was made
but does not support a conclusion at the current sample size or budget; and
\emph{conjecture} marks a reading we have not tested. Unless stated otherwise,
all numbers below are single runs, which is the main limitation of this section.

\subsection{What did not work}
\label{subsec:whatdidnotwork}

\paragraph{Odd symmetry of the proximal field.} The plain $\ell_1$ ConvScCP is
\emph{exactly} an odd operator, so half of the generated MNIST samples came out
colour-inverted (\Cref{subsubsec:oddsym}). This is architectural, holds at
initialisation, and is not an optimisation artefact. We tried two zero-initialised
fixes: a learned convolutional bias in the analysis operator (\texttt{w\_bias}),
and an asymmetric two-threshold dual prox
$\operatorname{clamp}(u,-\lambda^-,\lambda^+)$, which is the exact prox of
$\lambda^+\max(x,0)+\lambda^-\max(-x,0)$ and therefore also preserves the proximal
reading. Both break the parity, but we kept only \texttt{w\_bias}: it acts on the
input dependence of the map rather than on a static threshold, and it leaves a
single mechanism to explain in the paper. \emph{Verdict: confirmed (the symmetry),
fixed (the model).}

% \paragraph{Plain $x$-prediction collapses onto the data mean.} Training
% $D_\theta$ with an unweighted $\|D_\theta - x_1\|^2$ objective produces a constant
% generator: the network ignores $x_t$ and outputs the class mean
% ($\texttt{std\_gen}\approx 0.02$ against a true per-dimension standard deviation of
% $0.54$). The velocity-space weighting of \eqref{eq:xpred_loss} removes this in
% pixel space and is what we use throughout. The mechanism, however, is not the one
% we first assumed. We had attributed the collapse to the loss weighting; a control
% in which only the loss space changes (same network, $x$-prediction output, $v$-space
% loss --- the exact recipe advocated in \cite{li2026back}) collapsed
% \emph{identically} to the unweighted version on a degenerate target (a single MNIST
% class in a VAE latent). Diversity is decided at \emph{small} $t$, where the ODE
% starts from noise: if the network predicts the mean there, all trajectories merge
% immediately and never re-separate. At small $t$ the target $x_1$ has low variance
% and the $1/(1-t)^2$ weight is $\approx 1$, so the reweighting arrives too late to
% help. What decides is whether the regression target \emph{contains} $x_0$:
% $v$-prediction, whose target $x_1-x_0$ inherits the full variance of the Gaussian
% source at every $t$, is immune. \emph{Verdict: confirmed; our first explanation
% (``the loss space is the lever'') refuted by the control.} A partial fix through a
% forced skip, $\hat{x}_1 = x_t + (1-t)\,\mathcal{N}_\theta(x_t,t)$, recovers part of
% the diversity ($\texttt{std\_gen}\ 0.018 \to 0.193$) but not all of it ($0.576$ for
% $v$-prediction), and compensating its implicit $(1-t)^2$ reweighting exactly made
% things \emph{worse}: the correcting weight reaches $\times 400$ near $t=1$, a
% handful of samples then dominate the batch gradient, and gradient clipping discards
% precisely the small-$t$ signal that protects against the collapse. Equivalence in
% expectation is not equivalence under SGD.

\paragraph{Naked $x_1$-prediction is a learnability failure, not a loss problem.}
A control model (\texttt{SmallUNetX1}, strictly identical to the \texttt{SmallUNet}
baseline except for its output parameterisation) predicts the conditional mean
\emph{for every digit and every $t$}, including at $t=0.8$ where the conditional
variance $\mathrm{Var}(x_1\mid x_t)\approx 0.10$ shows the information is present.
Bounded reweightings (uniform, min-SNR) do not fix it. What distinguishes
$v$-prediction is that it is $x_1$-prediction \emph{preconditioned}: writing
$\hat{x}_1 = x_t + (1-t)\mathcal{N}_\theta$ puts a skip \emph{outside} the network,
so the network only has to produce a residual correlated with its input, instead of
synthesising a whole image through its bottleneck. This is the EDM-style
preconditioning argument, and it is also why our unrolled models are well behaved
in $x$-prediction: the ScCP recursion re-injects $z=x_t$ at every primal step, so
the skip is built into the algorithm. \emph{Verdict: confirmed, with a caveat} ---
part of this diagnosis was run under the buggy OT coupling of
\Cref{subsec:otbug}, and although the mechanism (missing skip) is coupling-independent,
the strength of the effect should be re-measured before being quoted as a general
claim about $x_1$-prediction.

\paragraph{Latent-space Flow Matching.} We tried running the unrolled flow in the
latent space of a light convolutional (mildly variational) autoencoder: each
$28\times28$ image is encoded to a $(c_{\text{lat}},7,7)$ code, the flow is matched
on the codes, and the decoder is used only for display. This was motivated by the
multiscale structure a UNet gets for free and the ScCP does not
(\Cref{subsubsec:vs_unet}). It consistently produced blurrier digits than the
pixel-space model, and the ScCP iterations converged markedly worse. Our reading,
offered as a \emph{likely cause} rather than a measured fact, is that the
analytical $\ell_1$ dual prox is no longer an appropriate prior in that domain:
sparsity is a meaningful regulariser on a pixel or wavelet representation, but
autoencoder codes are dense entangled features for which an $\ell_1$ clipping is
not a meaningful proximal step, so the unrolled solver loses the variational
structure it relies on. A second, more decisive objection is that this design moves
the credit for sample quality from the proximal architecture to the decoder, which
defeats the purpose of the study. \emph{Verdict: abandoned; all reported results are
in pixel space.} \emph{Conjecture:} a structured latent (wavelet or sparse-coding
compatible) rather than a learned dense one would be the way to retry this.

\paragraph{Weight sharing and the choice of algorithm family.} Tying
$(W,V,\tau,\sigma,\theta_{\text{prox}})$ across the $K$ unrolled iterations, which
would allow a variable inference depth, costs a large and consistent amount of
final loss on two moons (shared models stagnate above $2.1$ where per-layer models
reach $[1.75,1.95]$) and does not recover on MNIST. Similarly, ScCP never
outperformed plain DFB on two moons despite its stronger convergence guarantees.
\emph{Verdict: confirmed (gap in the loss); we abandoned shared UNNs.}

\subsection{Warm-starting the dual variable across ODE steps}
\label{subsec:warmstart}

At sampling time, each velocity evaluation restarts the unrolled recursion from
$u^{(0)}=0$, so the dual state built at the previous Euler step is discarded. Since
consecutive ODE steps solve nearly identical denoising problems, carrying $u$
forward is the natural warm start, and it is a diagnostic a UNet cannot even
express. We implemented it both post hoc (a $\gamma$-scaled carry at sampling time)
and at training time, through the latent self-conditioning trick of RIN
\cite{jabri2023rin}: with probability $0.9$, a no-grad cold pass on the previous
state supplies $u_{\text{prev}}$.

The result depends entirely on $K$, and the mechanism is a property of the
\emph{learned} weights rather than of the algorithm. On MNIST (digit $0$, $200$
epochs, OT coupling, mini-FID of \Cref{subsec:mnist_fid}, whose floor is
$\approx 4$):

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
$K$ & baseline / cold & self-conditioned / warm & effect \\
\midrule
$20$ & $23.8$  & $57.3$   & $-141\%$ \\
$6$  & $86.2$  & $64.0$   & $+26\%$ \\
$3$  & $301.5$ & $1059.1$ & $-251\%$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

At $K=20$ the channel is structurally closed: the unrolled recursion contracts the
dual initialisation whatever we do (the cold/warm divergence of $u$ after $20$
iterations is $8.5\times10^{-3}$, and $6.1\times10^{-4}$ for the self-conditioned
model, which learned to be \emph{more} forgetful). We first read this as exposure
bias --- the model sees a cold $u_{\text{prev}}$ during training and a warm one at
inference --- and refuted it with a third sampler that recomputes $u_{\text{prev}}$
by a cold pass on the previous state, reproducing the training condition exactly:
it gives $54.98$ against $55.0$ for the recursive sampler, i.e.\ the recursion adds
nothing. At $K=6$, where the truncated recursion retains $50\%$ of its dual
initialisation instead of $0.85\%$, the transfer does pay ($-26\%$ FID) --- but the
same control shows the gain comes from \emph{conditioning} on a dual computed at
the previous state, not from chaining along the trajectory. And even at its best,
warm starting never beats the cheaper option of unrolling further: $64.0$ at $K=6$
against $23.8$ for the plain $K=20$ baseline. On AFHQ ($K=10$, an open channel by
the same criterion) the paired run is blurrier and less diverse in both regimes.
\emph{Verdict: refuted as a useful lever} (one success in four trained pairs, in a
narrow window around $K=6$, always dominated by simply taking more iterations);
\emph{confirmed} that a per-layer dual-state diagnostic is available in this
architecture and not in a UNet.

\subsection{Is the unrolled network a local-score (ELS) machine?}
\label{subsec:els}

Kamb and Ganguli \cite{kamb2025analytic} show that the creativity of convolutional
diffusion models is largely explained by an analytic \emph{equivariant local score}
(ELS) machine: a denoiser that selects, independently at each position, the best
matching patch of the training set and stitches the result back by overlapping
aggregation. Their claim is quantitative --- the ELS machine predicts the trained
network's denoiser with $r^2\approx 0.94$, against $r^2\approx 0.61$ for the
memorising (ideal-score, IS) machine. Our ConvScCP is local and translation
equivariant by construction, which makes the question direct: is it an ELS machine,
and does that survive the FM framework and the OT coupling?

Answering it required reproducing their pipeline, porting our architecture into
their score-matching codebase, and porting their ResNet into ours, giving a $2\times2$
design (architecture $\times$ framework). Our first answer was that
ELS-predictability is a property of \emph{score matching}: both architectures were
ELS machines under score matching and neither was under Flow Matching. That answer
was wrong, and was corrected in two steps. First, NIFTY \cite{chatillon2025nifty}
derives the closed-form FM velocity on patches and shows it is the same object as
Kamb's Eq.~(5) up to a time reparameterisation, i.e.\ theory predicts that FM does
\emph{not} break the mechanism. Second, rebuilding the ELS machine faithfully (whole
patch with Gaussian fold, FM kernel) and --- the decisive point --- comparing
\emph{denoisers along the model's own trajectory} instead of \emph{integrated end
points} flipped every null result: ScCP-FM reaches $r^2 = 0.83$ against $0.66$ for
IS, ResNet-FM $0.85$ against $0.69$, with the calibrated patch size going
coarse-to-fine ($P=11$ at high noise to $P=3$ at low noise), exactly Kamb's
signature. The earlier null was a measurement artefact: at a denoiser fidelity of
$0.85$, integrating the ELS machine independently from the same $x_0$ diverges from
the model by ODE sensitivity and produces a different digit, even though the two
denoisers agree pixel-for-pixel on the model's path ($\cos = 1.00$ at $t=0.84$).
We also cross-checked our ELS implementation against the authors' own function
($\cos = 0.96$--$0.99$). \emph{Verdict: refuted (our ``FM breaks ELS'' claim);
confirmed that locality plus equivariance yields an ELS machine irrespective of the
framework.}

The comparison that survives is a different one. Across three architectures under
matched training, the ELS $r^2$ saturates near $1$ for all of them and therefore
does not discriminate; the discriminant is the \emph{gap} $r^2_{\text{ELS}} -
r^2_{\text{IS}}$, i.e.\ the distance to memorisation, and it orders the
architectures monotonically by locality: ScCP $0.72 \gg$ ResNet $0.17 \gg$ UNet
$0.02$, with the ELS-favouring gap emerging only for $t > 0.3$, where local mosaic
assembly and global memorisation start to differ. Kamb's UNet, with three pooling
stages and a global receptive field, is essentially indistinguishable from the
memorising machine on this scale. We also checked, since the closed form is derived
under independent coupling, that ELS-predictability survives OT coupling: it does.
Whether OT \emph{sharpens} it is \emph{inconclusive} --- an early single run
suggested it did ($0.83\to0.90$), the controlled three-architecture comparison did
not reproduce the effect, and we removed the claim. All of this rests on one run per
cell and eight seeds. \emph{Verdict: confirmed (locality orders the
creativity--memorisation position); inconclusive (any effect of the coupling).}

\subsection{Non-expansivity, convexity, and what actually drives recombination}
\label{subsec:lipschitz}

A tempting story about our architecture is that its convex-proximal structure is
what limits it: at convergence, an $\ell_1$ ScCP layer is the proximity operator of
a convex function, hence firmly non-expansive, whereas the exact target
$D^\star_t = \mathbb{E}[x_1\mid x_t]$ has Jacobian $\sigma_t^{-2}\mathrm{Cov}(x_1\mid
x_t)$, whose eigenvalues \emph{exceed} $1$ between modes of a multimodal
distribution. The target would then be structurally outside the model class. This
is testable, and we tested it. Measuring the local Lipschitz constant by exact power
iteration on the target and by finite differences on trained models, under
independent coupling and on held-out data: the trained ScCP ($K=6$, \texttt{w\_bias},
LNO) reaches $L\approx 3.9$, far from the value $1$ the idealised argument predicts.
Truncation at finite $K$, the analysis bias, and the velocity parameterisation each
take the model out of the firmly non-expansive class. Worse for the story, the
Lipschitz profiles of ScCP, ResNet and UNet are nearly identical (at the peak
$t\approx0.32$, $3.5$ against $5.3$): a $25$--$35\%$ imprint, correct in direction
but far too small to explain any quality gap. \emph{Verdict: refuted.} The only
regime where the argument could still bite is a near-proximal ScCP at large $K$,
which we did not test --- and which a further objection weakens: with independent
per-layer weights, large $K$ does not produce a contractive fixed-point iteration,
so ``$K\to\infty$ recovers an exact prox'' does not hold for our parameterisation.

Two clean results came out of the same series. First, \emph{locality controls
expansivity}: on the analytic target, the Lipschitz constant is monotone in the
patch size $P$ at fixed $t$ ($1.5$, $2.4$, $2.8$, $8.5$, $18.5$ for
$P = 3, 7, 11, 27$ and global, at $t=0.275$, on the same points $x_t$), and trained
ScCP models reproduce the hierarchy through their receptive field ($k{=}9,K{=}20$
against $k{=}3,K{=}6$: $3.44$ against $1.93$ at $t=0.16$). \emph{Verdict:
confirmed.} Second, and against our own expectation, \emph{expansivity does not
drive memorisation}: sweeping $P$, the Lipschitz constant grows by a factor of six
while the distance from generated samples to the training set does not move
(ratio $1.2$--$1.4$ times the distance of genuine held-out digits, i.e.\ all patch
denoisers generalise, including $P=27$ whose patch is nearly the whole image), and
memorisation appears as a \emph{cliff} at the global denoiser ($0.03$), not as a
slope. \emph{Verdict: refuted.}

What then is the lever? A deconfounding control with five denoisers isolates it.
Translation equivariance alone is not it: a global denoiser trained with translated
copies of the data looks novel by nearest-neighbour distance to the training set
(ratio $1.12$) but sits at distance $0.42$ from the \emph{augmented} set --- it
memorises translated digits. Receptive-field size is not it either: $P=27$ with
overlapping aggregation remains novel. What separates the two is the \emph{fold}:
$P=27$ with overlapping windows generates images far from both sets, and $P=27$
without it (central pixel only) memorises. The creative lever is therefore the
overlapping local aggregation, which lets patches from \emph{different} training
images be recombined into a mosaic that matches no training image, translated or
not. \emph{Verdict: confirmed, with a methodological warning} --- distance to the
training set alone is not a valid novelty measure in the presence of an equivariance;
it must be measured against the orbit. This connects directly to the proximal
reading of the report: through the Gribonval--Nikolova result \cite{gribonval2011should}
that any Gaussian MMSE denoiser is a proximity operator (of a generally
\emph{non-convex} penalty), the ELS
machine is a non-parametric patch prox and ScCP a learned parametric compression of
one. We give this bridge as an \emph{interpretation}, not as a proof about our
trained network.

\subsection{Different choices of proximal operators}
\label{subsec:proximaloperators}


\subsection{A prototyping bench: denoising at fixed noise levels}
\label{subsec:denoiseprobe}

Full generative training is too slow to explore an architecture space (see
\Cref{subsec:cost}), so we built a cheap surrogate: the same FM objective restricted
to a finite set of noise levels $t$, which is exactly a denoising benchmark, plus an
optimal \emph{linear} (Wiener) denoiser measured on the same split as a reference
($\mathrm{nmse}=0.2213$; we quote the measured value, not the analytic one, which is
optimistic by plug-in bias). Two lessons came out of it.

First, \emph{capacity is inert and the block family decides}. Multiplying the
parameters of a torchcfm UNet by four buys $2.4\%$; the same flatness had already
been observed on ScCP, where the number of dual channels \texttt{ic} is essentially
inert and doubling $K$ buys $5\%$. At matched capacity, ScCP ($1.25$M, $2$h$09$)
scores $0.2086$ against $0.2093$ for Kamb's minimal UNet ($2.11$M, $12.7$ min) ---
within the bootstrap error bar --- while a modern residual UNet that is
\emph{smaller} ($1.11$M) reaches $0.1929$ in $21$ min. \emph{Verdict: confirmed} ---
at this budget the unrolled network buys the quality of a minimal UNet for roughly
ten times the GPU time, and is about $10\%$ behind a modern one. This is, to date,
our most direct architectural comparison, and it does not favour unrolling on
quality per unit of compute.

Second, and this is a warning about the bench itself, \emph{an aggregated denoising
error is blind to the regime that decides sharpness}. The MSE-optimal denoiser is
$\mathbb{E}[x_1\mid x_t]$, which is blurry by construction, and the average over
$t\in\{0.2,\dots,0.8\}$ is dominated by $t=0.2$ (nmse $0.48$ against $0.068$ at
$t=0.8$), i.e.\ by coarse structure. Sharpness is decided as $t\to1$, and this is
exactly where ScCP stalls: $\mathrm{nmse}(0.95) = 0.046$ against $0.0066$ for the
residual UNet, a factor of seven, on models that are tied on the aggregate. This
yields a falsifiable prediction we did not have time to test: Kamb's minimal UNet,
which stalls too ($0.036$), should also produce the blurry ``blobs'' we observe on
AFHQ, whereas the residual UNet should not. \emph{Verdict: confirmed (the blind
spot); prediction untested.} Two protocol requirements follow: never rank runs of
different step budgets together (at $3000$ steps every model is still above the
linear baseline and the bench ranks optimisation speed only), and never select a
generative architecture on an aggregated denoising error.

\subsection{The computational cost of unrolling}
\label{subsec:cost}

Our tutors asked why ConvScCP training runs take roughly seven times longer than a
comparable UNet. The answer is neither the parameter count nor the arithmetic:
ScCP at $K=20$ costs $21$ GFLOPs per velocity evaluation, $1.5$ times the
\texttt{SmallUNet} and $6.5$ times \emph{less} than the torchcfm UNet that trains at
the same speed. It is GPU efficiency, about five times worse: the unrolled recursion
decomposes the computation into $\approx 2000$ tiny CUDA kernels (against $213$ for
\texttt{SmallUNet}), all at full $28\times28$ resolution since there is no
downsampling, with low-arithmetic-intensity $9\times9$ convolutions between $1$ and
$\texttt{ic}$ channels and memory-bound elementwise primal--dual algebra on the dual
variable at every iteration. The signature is that dividing the kernel size from
$9$ to $3$ divides the FLOPs by eight but the runtime by only $1.5$. A corollary
worth stating, since it misled us: \texttt{nvidia-smi} reported $99\%$ utilisation
throughout, but that field measures the fraction of time during which at least one
kernel is resident and is blind to throughput --- ScCP and \texttt{SmallUNet} show
the same SM occupancy ($80\%$ against $82\%$) at $129$ against $620$ GFLOP/s.
\texttt{torch.compile} did not help (it was slower). \emph{Verdict: confirmed; this
is a structural property of unrolled architectures on current hardware, and it
bounds what an internship-scale compute budget can explore.}

\subsection{Scaling up: CIFAR-10, and what a step budget buys}
\label{subsec:scaling}

The natural next dataset after MNIST was CIFAR-10, with the OT-CFM UNet
\cite{tong2024improving} as the reference backbone --- a clean comparison, since
that paper contributes the coupling and not the architecture. Three findings, none
of which is about the architecture, decided how we spent the remaining compute.

\paragraph{The transport is learned long before the sharpness.} Comparing our
$50$-epoch UNet with the official $400$k-step OT-CFM weights from the \emph{same}
$x_0$, both produce the \emph{same scene} --- the same car, the same dog, the same
composition --- and differ only in sharpness. The gap is convergence, not capacity
or architecture. \emph{Verdict: confirmed, single comparison.}

\paragraph{Neither the loss nor early EMA samples measure quality.} Our ConvScCP
reaches an FM loss of $0.146$ against $0.138$ for the reference under OT --- a $6\%$
gap that carries the entire distance between an FID of about $4.8$ and one above
$50$. The loss is dominated by the irreducible conditional variance and cannot
resolve it. Independently, sampling from EMA weights (decay $0.9999$, time constant
$\approx10$k steps) at $10$k steps shows blurry red-black images while the raw
weights already generate structured scenes: roughly $37\%$ of the EMA average is
still random initialisation at that point. Judging a run at $10$k steps from its EMA
samples --- which we did --- concludes failure on a model that works.
\emph{Verdict: confirmed (both traps).}

\paragraph{Below a threshold in steps, a run is not evaluable.} A ConvScCP sized for
CIFAR ($36.5$M parameters) ran at $0.22$ it/s, i.e.\ an ETA of $514$ hours, and was
killed by preemption on a shared GPU. We therefore pivoted to AFHQ-cats at
$32\times32$, a single-mode aligned distribution of $5\,653$ images that is $20$
times cheaper per step. There, incomplete runs ($45$--$55$k steps) produce blobs
while the one run taken to $165$k steps produces genuine cats, at \emph{lower}
capacity than two of the failing runs. A delayed-start diagnostic --- integrating
from a real $x_{t_0}$ and locating the $t_0^\star$ below which samples stop being
sharp --- localises the failure: the field is learned from $t=1$ backwards, and the
interval $[0,0.5]$, which decides global structure, converges last. The two
\emph{largest} models ($9.6$M and $10.4$M) are the worst at a fixed step budget:
capacity is counter-productive when it is not paid for in steps. A
train/validation denoising comparison confirms the reading, with a generalisation
gap statistically indistinguishable from zero on four checkpoints (including one at
$2500$ equivalent epochs): these models \emph{underfit}, so adding data would change
nothing. \emph{Verdict: confirmed} --- with the practical rule that no AFHQ run below
$\approx150$k steps should be used to conclude anything about an architecture, and
the acknowledgement that this rule invalidates most of the scaling comparisons we ran.

\paragraph{Prepared but not run.} The RGB extension to ImageNet-32 required changing
only the analysis kernel from $(\texttt{ic},1,9,9)$ to $(\texttt{ic},3,9,9)$ --- the
dual variable, the prox, the schedule and the LNO bound are colour-agnostic, and the
colour mixing is absorbed by $W$, which is a small illustration of the fact that
Chambolle--Pock is defined on any Hilbert space. The code is smoke-tested; the
dataset was never available on the machine, so no result is reported.

